WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.405
SQL injection risk usually enters a Go
application through a design shortcut:

2
00:00:04.485 --> 00:00:07.917
query text is assembled from data whose
origin is external, unclear,

3
00:00:07.997 --> 00:00:09.416
or only loosely controlled.

4
00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:12.869
A developer may be trying to add a filter,
a search feature, a report,

5
00:00:12.949 --> 00:00:15.375
a sort order, or an administrative tool
quickly.

6
00:00:15.455 --> 00:00:19.123
If the query-building code treats
caller-influenced text as SQL syntax,

7
00:00:19.203 --> 00:00:21.925
the database boundary becomes harder to
reason about.

8
00:00:22.005 --> 00:00:24.904
The source of the data can be broader than
a public web form.

9
00:00:24.984 --> 00:00:28.415
Values may arrive through REST APIs,
internal APIs, message queues,

10
00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:32.459
uploaded files, scheduled imports,
command-line tools, support dashboards,

11
00:00:32.539 --> 00:00:34.906
analytics features, or another
microservice.

12
00:00:34.986 --> 00:00:38.891
A value may also be read from a database
table that was populated earlier by user

13
00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:39.503
activity.

14
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:41.240
Once data is stored or forwarded,

15
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.160
it can look internal even when its origin
was not trusted.

16
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.843
Risk is also not limited to authentication
screens.

17
00:00:46.923 --> 00:00:50.532
Filters, search boxes, date ranges, sort
fields, pagination controls,

18
00:00:50.612 --> 00:00:53.275
report builders, bulk upload mapping,
export tools,

19
00:00:53.355 --> 00:00:56.491
and optional query features all influence
database behavior.

20
00:00:56.571 --> 00:01:00.298
Some of these features feel operational
rather than security-sensitive,

21
00:01:00.378 --> 00:01:02.626
which is why they deserve deliberate
review.

22
00:01:02.706 --> 00:01:06.730
A defensive architecture traces where data
comes from before deciding how it may

23
00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:07.756
influence a query.

24
00:01:07.836 --> 00:01:11.800
Teams should identify the places where
request data becomes an internal model,

25
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.188
where internal models become database
calls,

26
00:01:14.268 --> 00:01:16.634
and where dynamic query structure is
selected.

27
00:01:16.714 --> 00:01:18.608
That map does not need to be elaborate.

28
00:01:18.688 --> 00:01:22.948
It needs to make the database boundary
visible enough that safe construction becomes

29
00:01:23.028 --> 00:01:24.980
consistent across everyday features.
